{
    "title": "Airworthiness Directives; The Boeing Company Airplanes",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 09, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-09/pdf/2025-22363.pdf",
    "summary": "The Federal Aviation Administration has issued new safety rules for Boeing 787s and certain Airbus A300 models that require targeted inspections and repairs to address potential structural issues. The rules take effect January 13, 2026.\n\nFor Boeing 787\u20138, \u20139, and \u201310 aircraft, the agency is mandating internal and external inspections of the forward pressure bulkhead after reports of assembly gaps and related concerns. The goal is to catch early cracking before it threatens the aircraft\u2019s structure. The agency kept the proposal largely unchanged after public comments, correcting a minor labeling error and clarifying that certain steps are optional. Requests to expand the inspection scope, require re-inspections, or add new reporting were declined, though the agency left the door open to future changes if new data emerges. The inspection is expected to take about 10 labor hours and cost roughly $850 per airplane, with 135 U.S.-registered jets affected. Repair costs will vary based on findings and may be partially covered by manufacturer warranties.\n\nSeparately, the agency updated an earlier rule for Airbus A300 series airplanes to continue required wing inspections and, when needed, modifications. This update adds some aircraft and removes others to better match the fleet at risk, with the aim of preventing cracking that could weaken the wing over time.\n\nWho benefits? Passengers, crews, and communities gain from earlier detection of issues that could compromise safety. Airlines and manufacturers get clear, focused instructions that target known risks, which can help avoid larger disruptions later.\n\nWho may feel the downsides? Airlines will face inspection costs and potential downtime, and smaller operators may feel the impact more acutely when repairs are needed. Maintenance teams will have added workload. The agency says the overall economic impact is limited and that the steps are necessary to keep these aircraft safe over their service life.\n\nBottom line: these are targeted, near-term actions to find and fix specific risks on well-used aircraft types, balancing safety needs with practical costs while remaining open to future adjustments if new evidence warrants it.",
